<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- No backend of our own, no analytics, no third-party SDK. What the app
       does send off the device is the reader's own library — states, chapter
       progress, ratings, notes — to their MangaBaka account, under the token
       they pasted from mangabaka.org. Apple's definition of "collected" is
       "transmitted off the device in a way that allows you and/or your
       third-party partners to access it for longer than servicing the
       request", and MangaBaka keeps it, so it is declared: a user ID (the
       account the token names) and other user content, linked to the
       reader, for the app's function only, never for tracking. Declared
       2026-09-11 before the first submission; the App Store Connect
       questionnaire must say the same.

       Every host this build actually contacts. Re-checked 2026-09-14, and
       the earlier method was wrong twice over — worth saying, because the
       same method would miss the same things again.

       It was: grep every network client for its base URL. That finds only
       hosts spelled out in the source, so it missed every host that arrives
       inside a response and is fetched because the payload said so. Cover
       art is the whole of that class and it is the app's largest single
       source of requests: cdn.mangabaka.dev and images.mangabaka.dev (cover
       art, in the app and in the widget extension, no reader identifier
       sent) and s4.anilist.co (cast portraits), none of which appeared in
       this list or in `PrivacyManifestTests.ContactedHosts` before today.
       covers.openlibrary.org (fallback cover art) was in the test's list and
       not in this comment — the two had already drifted, which is what the
       test was added to prevent.

       And comic.naver.com is no longer contacted at all: the Naver adapter
       was deleted on 2026-09-14 (Q8). The name survives in
       `ReleaseSource.scopedHosts`, where it classifies a link the reader may
       tap, so a grep still finds it — a mention is not a request.

       The list, then: api.mangabaka.org (the account above);
       cdn.mangabaka.dev and images.mangabaka.dev (cover art);
       graphql.anilist.co, s4.anilist.co and shikimori.io (cast data and
       portraits, no reader identifier sent); itunes.apple.com,
       www.googleapis.com and books.google.com (volume search,
       unauthenticated, no reader identifier sent); covers.openlibrary.org
       (fallback covers); www.webtoons.com (release feeds); the seven
       GigaViewer magazine hosts — tonarinoyj.jp, shonenjumpplus.com,
       comic-days.com, magcomi.com, shonenmagazine.com, comic-gardo.com,
       comic-earthstar.com — read as plain RSS with no request parameters at
       all; api.mangaupdates.com (release cadence, unauthenticated); and, from
       2026-09-14, three edition sources that replace what Naver, Amazon and
       Google Books gave us — cdn.animenewsnetwork.com (English volume dates
       and ISBNs), openlibrary.org (editions by ISBN, alongside the covers
       host already listed) and ndlsearch.ndl.go.jp (Japanese volumes). All
       three are unauthenticated, keyless, and receive a title or an ISBN and
       nothing about the reader. None of the non-MangaBaka hosts receives
       anything that identifies the reader.
       -->
  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>
  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>
  <!-- Required-reason APIs. Apple rejects an upload that uses one of these
       and does not declare it (ITMS-91053), and the check is on the binary,
       not on the source — so an undeclared use is found at submission time
       rather than here.

       Audited 2026-09-11, re-audited 2026-09-14 for the App Store decision.
       UserDefaults is still the only one this app touches. Twelve files use
       it — two more name it only in a comment, which is why the old count of
       eleven was both stale and arrived at the wrong way — all for the
       reader's own settings on their own device: title language, content
       rating, formats, blocked tags, onboarding state, reminders, saved
       lenses, recent searches. CA92.1 is the reason for exactly that:
       accessing information from the app itself, and no data is sent
       anywhere.

       Verified by finding the call, not by assuming: every UserDefaults use
       above, and the absence of the other three categories — file timestamps
       (no `attributesOfItem`, no `resourceValues`, no `creationDate` or
       `contentModificationDate` anywhere in MangaBaka/ or MangaBakaWidgets/),
       disk space (no `volumeAvailableCapacity`, `systemFreeSize` or
       `statfs`), system boot time (no `systemUptime`, no
       `mach_absolute_time`) and active keyboards (no `activeInputModes`, no
       `UITextInputMode`).

       Assumed, not verified: GRDB. It is the only third-party code linked in
       and it opens, renames and deletes SQLite files, which is where a file
       timestamp call would plausibly live. GRDB ships its own manifest
       (SourcePackages/checkouts/GRDB.swift/GRDB/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy,
       read 2026-09-14) declaring no collected data and no accessed API
       types, and Apple evaluates a package's own manifest — so this rests on
       GRDB's declaration rather than on anyone here having read its source.
       If ITMS-91053 ever names a category this app does not use, GRDB is the
       first place to look.

       Not declared here and not needed here: the widget extension. It is its
       own bundle and has no manifest of its own, which is correct only for
       as long as it uses no required-reason API — checked 2026-09-14, it
       uses none (no UserDefaults, no file attributes; it reads one JSON file
       out of the App Group container and fetches cover art). Adding one to
       MangaBakaWidgets is the fix if that ever changes. -->
  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>CA92.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
